in today's morning rounds a new virus  that has killed nine people and infected  hundreds more in China has now spread to  the US a man in his 30s from Washington  state is the first person in America to  be diagnosed with corona virus it's  called the man returned to the Seattle  area last week from a trip to Wuhan  that's a city in central China where the  outbreak began right now that patient is  in isolation in a hospital north of  Seattle our number-one priority is to  complete the identification of all the  patients contacts reach out to the  contacts and monitor their health we  also want to make enhanced screening is  already in place at three US airports  with two more being added this week our  doctor Tara Narula is here at the table  with more on this story you hear about  it you see the mask it's very disturbing  how worried should we be yeah and what  is it exactly so this is a form of a  corona virus we've known about Crona  viruses before this is a new strain this  is a virus that's found worldwide it  causes typically a mild or moderate  respiratory illness but it can be more  severe we saw that with stars and MERS  which are also forms of corona virus so  certainly the CDC is recommending that  we be cautious about this that we'd be  proactive because it is a new strain it  is contagious so interestingly they  think it does circulate in animals and  occasionally it can make the jump from  animals to humans which is what they  think happened in this case because the  initial cases were surrounding an area a  market a seafood animal market in the  wuhan area and there so they think that  it started in animals it was transmitted  to humans and now they're saying that in  fact it is spread between human to  humans and there's no vaccine right now  so how are people being treated there's  no vaccine the NIH is doing research to  develop a vaccine but that's a ways away  and so it's really supportive care at  this point which means you know fluids  rest pain medications medicines to lower  fever temperature how our health  officials tracking the spread of this  virus how are they looking for people  who may be infected well certainly with  this case they've sent you know a team  to Washington and they're doing a  traceback where they're looking at  everyone that this gentleman may have  come in contact with back in China on  the plane in the United States and they  have activated kind of their emergency  operations they're also in communication  with the whu-oh which is convening a  meeting today  to decide whether they're gonna declare  this a worldwide public health we see  these pictures of people wearing though  the kind of the masks out in public do  those do anything to prevent infection  well certainly we think that this is  spread by droplets so you know airborne  precautions like wearing a mask can help  also if you touch a surface kind of like  the flu it can be spread that way so  hand-washing disinfection  what are the symptoms Tara so it can be  anything from fever to cough respiratory  difficulties shortness of breath muscle  aches it can progress to pneumonia and  you know there have been some deaths  although the deaths that have occurred  have been in those who are older than 60  and also seemed to have underlying  medical conditions so I think the big  message here is not to panic the CDC is  saying that at this point the risk to  the general public is low so if you  haven't been to China or haven't been in  contact with somebody who's been to  China do you have reason to be concerned  I think the CDC is saying again the risk  is low so they said for any one kind of  sitting around there a dinner table this  is not something that you have to be  worried about but certainly because it  is a new strain and people don't have  immunity to it and strains can mutate  and change there they are worried and  that's why they're saying we need to be  prepared and proactive and take kind of  a widespread public health approach  which is what they're doing and and  they've seen this before they've done  this before in SARS and MERS so dr. Tara  Narula thanks  you
 

